    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Moke Reloads the Electric Beach Car, Mk2 to Make Buggies of Old Green With Envy\nThe EU's New Chinese Hybrid Vehicle Import Quota Kicks Off at 350,000 Vehicles per Year\n29 photos\nPhoto: MG\nPicture a Europe that is burning through approximately 1 billion euros every single day on trade deficits with the People's Republic of China. This isn't a hypothetical scenario, but the harsh reality of an increasingly uncompetitive continent. Instead of learning a thing or two from Chinese players, the suits in Brussels have decided to protect European economies and established automakers from cheaper and more tech-heavy Chinese hybrid imports.\nLast year alone, the deficit scaled to a staggering 360 billion euros. Surging vehicle shipments from Chinese manufacturers turned a macroeconomic headache into a crisis situation, and Chinese automakers are all too happy to aggressively ramp up exports in order to escape a brutal sales slump at home.\nThe European Union originally provided them with an inviting open door, only to then slap aggressive tariffs on Chinese battery-electric vehicles in 2024. That levy wasn't enough to fend off these newcomers, leaving the door open for hybrids. China exploited the regulatory oversight, capturing roughly a quarter of the entire hybrid market in Europe by August 2026.\nTotal vehicle imports, meanwhile, jumped nearly three-quarters through August 2026. Said information comes from Dataforce, cited by the peeps at Bloomberg, with the latter also reporting that established automaker watched their market share evaporate in thin air. Mercedes, for example, experienced an 8 percent drop during the third quarter of the current year.\nThe Volkswagen Group has it rough as well, slashing its profit forecast mere weeks after BMW issued a similar warning. In light of this pressure, both Germany and France lost patience. Ahead of emergency trade negotiations, Berlin and Paris demanded that Brussels expand its retaliatory arsenal. They even suggested that the European bloc consider severing trade ties with bad actors launching trade wars.\nPhoto: MG Both Germany and France further pressed for aggressive subsidy probes into foreign chemicals and plastics, which goes to show how desperate both economies are. In any case, that kind of political muscle does not go unnoticed. EU trade chief Maros Sefcovic met Chinese commerce minister Wang Wentao in Beijing, and their resulting agreement has produced a strict volume framework affecting Chinese hybrid vehicle imports.\nFor the initial 12 months, China would be hit by a 350,000-vehicle ceiling. Across a four-year enforcement window, the ceiling is planned to climb by 6 percent annually. Both trading blocs committed to respecting the WTO's guidelines throughout the process. As part of this deal, members of the European Economic Area, developing WTO members, Ukraine, and Kenya, specifically markets with negligible import shares, will be exempt.\nThis policy drags in self-charging hybrids and plug-in setups. In its current form, the document does not clarify whether shipments breaching the cap face outright border bans or steep punitive tariffs. Beijing, for its part, did not hand over such concessions without something to gain for itself.\nChina agreed to strip tariffs from select automotive parts while cutting duties in half for olive oil, ice cream, and shoes, which is a terribly interesting combo when you think about it. Also temporary, this package is worth approximately 4 billion euros. Bloomberg reports the EU's safeguard measure could take effect as soon as December 1, 2026.\nSefcovic is scheduled to return to Beijing early next year to audit early results. Whether a paper wall of 350,000 hybrids plus 6 percent yearly increases can halt a runaway freight train remains an open question, though.\nWhile this measure protects both European economies and legacy automakers, it does not fix what everyone and their dog currently detests about the Volkswagen Group and similar automakers. Think high prices, very questionable quality, increasingly poor aftersales services, and other compounding issues that made them lose European customers to Chinese brands in the first place.\nVia // Bloomberg\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEurope China Hybrid PHEV industry\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Is a One-Owner 1964 1/2 Mustang Convertible, and Fate Has Been So Cruel to It",
